  "discovery_metadata": {
    "doi": "10.1016/j.indcrop.2025.122014",
    "title": "Microbial-assisted upcycling of agricultural waste into dual-functional porous carbon for synergistic tetracycline decontamination and sustainable energy storage",
    "score": 9,
    "date": "2025-10-01",
    "abstract": "Converting agricultural waste into high-value materials is a promising strategy for concurrent environmental remediation and energy storage. Herein, we report a microbial-assisted route to convert soybean straw into hierarchical porous carbon (designated M800-Zn, where M = Myrothecium verrucaria ，800 for 800 ℃ Zn-dual-salt activation). Starting from the raw biochar (SSC, 7.66 m 2 g −1 , 0.1289 mg g −1 TC uptake), fungal pre-treatment followed by dual-salt activation boosts the BET area 317-fold to 2433.97 m 2 g −1 and the tetracycline adsorption capacity 1934-fold to 249.37 mg g −1 (pH 7), surpassing most reported carbons. When assembled into a symmetric supercapacitor, M800-Zn delivered 254.5 F g −1 at 0.5 A g −1 and retained 93.75 % capacitance after 5000 cycles at 2 A g −1 . This low-energy, 800 ℃ process (about 100 ℃ lower than conventional activation) thus provides a circular-economy-compatible material that simultaneously purifies antibiotic-contaminated water and sustains high-power energy storage. Schematic representation detailing the transformation of soybean straw into the high-performance porous carbon material M800-Zn through a combination of Myrothecium verrucaria microbial fermentation and chemical activation. This process results in a material with a high specific surface area and multi-stage pore structure, which is demonstrated to be effective for environmental remediation through its adsorption capacity for tetracycline hydrochloride, and for energy storage, as indicated by its specific capacitance when used as a supercapacitor electrode material. • M800-Zn made from agri-waste via microbial fermentation & mild activation. • Material owns a high surface area (2433.97 m² g - ¹) and hierarchical pore structure. • Efficient TC adsorption (249.37 mg g - ¹) at neutral pH for water remediation. • Remarkable capacitance of 254.5 F g - ¹ at 0.5 A g - ¹ with excellent cycling stability. • The symmetric cell retains 93.75 % of its initial capacitance after 5000 consecutive charge–discharge cycles.",
